1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,543
Picture a small,

2
00:00:01,543 --> 00:00:05,123
landlocked country of about 8.9 million

3
00:00:05,123 --> 00:00:06,210
people.

4
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:07,912
It has no coastline,

5
00:00:07,912 --> 00:00:10,058
no direct access to the sea,

6
00:00:10,058 --> 00:00:11,987
and it sits in the middle of

7
00:00:11,987 --> 00:00:14,132
a continent full of larger,

8
00:00:14,132 --> 00:00:15,696
louder neighbours.

9
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:16,521
And yet,

10
00:00:16,521 --> 00:00:18,156
in 2023,

11
00:00:18,156 --> 00:00:21,624
this country produced roughly 104,000

12
00:00:21,624 --> 00:00:24,185
dollars of economic output for every

13
00:00:24,185 --> 00:00:25,770
person living there.

14
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,187
That is almost twice Germany's figure,

15
00:00:28,187 --> 00:00:29,977
and about two and a half times

16
00:00:29,977 --> 00:00:32,963
the average across the European Union.

17
00:00:33,217 --> 00:00:36,567
How did Switzerland get this rich?

18
00:00:36,797 --> 00:00:39,014
The usual answers are banks,

19
00:00:39,014 --> 00:00:41,297
chocolate and watches.

20
00:00:41,387 --> 00:00:43,285
The real story is stranger,

21
00:00:43,285 --> 00:00:44,703
more deliberate,

22
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:47,106
and it comes with costs that rarely

23
00:00:47,106 --> 00:00:48,587
make the postcards.

24
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:51,655
This is The Hidden Economics.

25
00:00:51,917 --> 00:00:52,917
In this film,

26
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:55,224
we will follow the money through four

27
00:00:55,224 --> 00:00:56,337
forces.

28
00:00:56,367 --> 00:00:57,565
Stability,

29
00:00:57,565 --> 00:00:59,877
built over two centuries.

30
00:01:00,137 --> 00:01:00,842
Money,

31
00:01:00,842 --> 00:01:02,788
and the rise and fall of banking

32
00:01:02,788 --> 00:01:04,117
secrecy.

33
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:05,702
Specialisation,

34
00:01:05,702 --> 00:01:07,471
in products the rest of the world

35
00:01:07,471 --> 00:01:09,177
finds hard to copy.

36
00:01:09,347 --> 00:01:10,279
And skills,

37
00:01:10,279 --> 00:01:13,127
built in classrooms and workshops.

38
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:15,286
Then we will look at the bill:

39
00:01:15,286 --> 00:01:18,373
the hidden costs of being this successful.

40
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:25,477
Let's start with the numbers.

41
00:01:25,477 --> 00:01:28,328
Measured as output per person,

42
00:01:28,328 --> 00:01:30,631
Switzerland sits well above its

43
00:01:30,631 --> 00:01:31,737
neighbours.

44
00:01:31,737 --> 00:01:32,535
Austria,

45
00:01:32,535 --> 00:01:33,608
Germany,

46
00:01:33,608 --> 00:01:36,331
France and Italy all trail behind,

47
00:01:36,331 --> 00:01:39,407
and so does the United States.

48
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:43,390
And this is not a lucky year.

49
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,474
Follow the data back to the start

50
00:01:45,474 --> 00:01:46,756
of the century,

51
00:01:46,756 --> 00:01:49,362
and Switzerland stays ahead of Germany and

52
00:01:49,362 --> 00:01:52,619
the European Union average in every single

53
00:01:52,619 --> 00:01:56,650
year from 2000 to 2023.

54
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,100
Whatever is driving Swiss wealth,

55
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:00,947
it is structural.

56
00:02:01,183 --> 00:02:03,481
A quick but important note on what

57
00:02:03,481 --> 00:02:05,083
this number means.

58
00:02:05,083 --> 00:02:07,184
Output per person measures how much an

59
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:08,692
economy produces,

60
00:02:08,692 --> 00:02:11,393
divided by everyone who lives there.

61
00:02:11,613 --> 00:02:13,069
It is not the same as the

62
00:02:13,069 --> 00:02:15,752
money in an ordinary person's pocket,

63
00:02:15,752 --> 00:02:18,019
and it is measured in US dollars,

64
00:02:18,019 --> 00:02:20,283
so exchange rates move it too.

65
00:02:20,523 --> 00:02:21,824
We will come back to what that

66
00:02:21,824 --> 00:02:24,283
means for everyday prices later.

67
00:02:24,513 --> 00:02:26,484
But as a measure of how productive

68
00:02:26,484 --> 00:02:27,844
an economy is,

69
00:02:27,844 --> 00:02:29,627
it tells a clear story.

70
00:02:35,283 --> 00:02:37,925
The first force is stability,

71
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:39,713
and it is old.

72
00:02:39,923 --> 00:02:41,788
In 1815,

73
00:02:41,788 --> 00:02:44,238
at the end of the Napoleonic wars,

74
00:02:44,238 --> 00:02:47,159
Europe's great powers formally recognised

75
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:48,883
Swiss neutrality.

76
00:02:49,113 --> 00:02:49,577
Then,

77
00:02:49,577 --> 00:02:51,604
In 1848,

78
00:02:51,604 --> 00:02:54,291
a new constitution turned a loose group

79
00:02:54,291 --> 00:02:56,953
of cantons into a federal state,

80
00:02:56,953 --> 00:02:58,394
with a parliament,

81
00:02:58,394 --> 00:03:01,437
a government and a federal court.

82
00:03:01,683 --> 00:03:05,103
That independent streak never went away.

83
00:03:05,123 --> 00:03:07,043
Switzerland is not a member of the

84
00:03:07,043 --> 00:03:08,603
European Union.

85
00:03:08,683 --> 00:03:09,323
Instead,

86
00:03:09,323 --> 00:03:11,972
it manages its relationship with the bloc

87
00:03:11,972 --> 00:03:14,598
through a web of bilateral agreements,

88
00:03:14,598 --> 00:03:17,185
negotiated sector by sector.

89
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:19,570
Why does this matter for money?

90
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:22,430
Because capital hates surprises.

91
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:24,626
A country where rules change slowly,

92
00:03:24,626 --> 00:03:25,693
and in public,

93
00:03:25,693 --> 00:03:27,850
is a country where a factory,

94
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,681
a laboratory or a savings account can

95
00:03:30,681 --> 00:03:32,370
plan for decades.

96
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,236
Predictability is hard to measure,

97
00:03:35,236 --> 00:03:37,289
but it may be the most valuable

98
00:03:37,289 --> 00:03:40,171
thing Switzerland has ever produced.

99
00:03:45,817 --> 00:03:48,299
Stability attracted money,

100
00:03:48,299 --> 00:03:51,407
and Switzerland learned to protect it.

101
00:03:51,757 --> 00:03:53,712
In 1934,

102
00:03:53,712 --> 00:03:56,068
a new federal banking law made it

103
00:03:56,068 --> 00:03:58,895
a criminal offence for bankers to reveal

104
00:03:58,895 --> 00:04:00,437
their clients' secrets.

105
00:04:00,657 --> 00:04:01,420
Before that,

106
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:04,161
confidentiality had been a matter of civil

107
00:04:04,161 --> 00:04:04,957
law.

108
00:04:05,147 --> 00:04:05,652
Now,

109
00:04:05,652 --> 00:04:08,646
breaking it could mean prosecution.

110
00:04:08,883 --> 00:04:10,060
For decades,

111
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,033
discretion became part of the Swiss brand.

112
00:04:13,033 --> 00:04:15,806
But secrecy that protects privacy can also

113
00:04:15,806 --> 00:04:18,152
protect people hiding money from their own

114
00:04:18,152 --> 00:04:18,984
governments,

115
00:04:18,984 --> 00:04:21,503
and pressure from abroad kept growing.

116
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:24,690
The turning point came quietly.

117
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,564
In 2018,

118
00:04:26,564 --> 00:04:28,391
under a global standard for tax

119
00:04:28,391 --> 00:04:29,385
transparency,

120
00:04:29,385 --> 00:04:32,226
Switzerland automatically shared financial

121
00:04:32,226 --> 00:04:34,458
account information with other countries

122
00:04:34,458 --> 00:04:35,870
for the first time.

123
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:38,559
That first exchange covered around two

124
00:04:38,559 --> 00:04:40,130
million accounts.

125
00:04:40,430 --> 00:04:42,682
The law on secrecy still exists at

126
00:04:42,682 --> 00:04:43,110
home,

127
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:45,588
but foreign tax authorities now get the

128
00:04:45,588 --> 00:04:46,639
data.

129
00:04:46,883 --> 00:04:49,176
There is another way Switzerland attracts

130
00:04:49,176 --> 00:04:49,781
money,

131
00:04:49,781 --> 00:04:51,449
and it does not require a bank

132
00:04:51,449 --> 00:04:52,863
account at all.

133
00:04:52,863 --> 00:04:54,285
In times of crisis,

134
00:04:54,285 --> 00:04:56,346
investors look for places to park their

135
00:04:56,346 --> 00:04:58,612
wealth that are politically stable and

136
00:04:58,612 --> 00:05:01,273
unlikely to inflate it away.

137
00:05:01,373 --> 00:05:03,479
Economists call that a safe haven,

138
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:05,411
and the Swiss franc is often described

139
00:05:05,411 --> 00:05:06,398
as one.

140
00:05:06,650 --> 00:05:08,930
Safety has a side effect.

141
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,027
When investors trust a country,

142
00:05:11,027 --> 00:05:12,599
they buy its currency,

143
00:05:12,599 --> 00:05:15,332
and a strong currency makes everything

144
00:05:15,332 --> 00:05:18,210
that country sells abroad more expensive.

145
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:19,697
On September 6th,

146
00:05:19,697 --> 00:05:21,098
2011,

147
00:05:21,098 --> 00:05:22,824
the Swiss National Bank drew a line

148
00:05:22,824 --> 00:05:24,000
in the sand.

149
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:26,356
It would no longer tolerate a euro

150
00:05:26,356 --> 00:05:28,430
worth less than one franc twenty.

151
00:05:28,683 --> 00:05:31,171
Defending that line meant buying foreign

152
00:05:31,171 --> 00:05:33,983
currency again and again.

153
00:05:34,233 --> 00:05:36,380
The central bank later said it could

154
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:39,003
only have been held with interventions of

155
00:05:39,003 --> 00:05:41,273
rapidly increasing size.

156
00:05:41,473 --> 00:05:43,029
On January 15th,

157
00:05:43,029 --> 00:05:44,430
2015,

158
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:46,543
it abandoned the minimum rate with

159
00:05:46,543 --> 00:05:48,103
immediate effect.

160
00:05:48,273 --> 00:05:49,590
The franc jumped,

161
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:52,071
and markets were caught off guard.

162
00:05:52,317 --> 00:05:55,126
The battle over the franc never really

163
00:05:55,126 --> 00:05:56,237
ended.

164
00:05:56,397 --> 00:05:58,600
In twenty twenty-two and twenty

165
00:05:58,600 --> 00:05:59,555
twenty-three,

166
00:05:59,555 --> 00:06:02,052
the central bank did the opposite:

167
00:06:02,052 --> 00:06:04,744
it sold foreign currency to strengthen the

168
00:06:04,744 --> 00:06:06,678
franc and make imports cheaper,

169
00:06:06,678 --> 00:06:08,747
to dampen inflation.

170
00:06:08,967 --> 00:06:10,982
In twenty twenty-three alone,

171
00:06:10,982 --> 00:06:14,707
it sold about 148 billion dollars worth.

172
00:06:14,847 --> 00:06:16,460
In twenty twenty-four,

173
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:17,838
it was buying again,

174
00:06:17,838 --> 00:06:21,640
but only about 1.4 billion dollars.

175
00:06:21,883 --> 00:06:24,550
Even Swiss banking is not immune to

176
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:25,673
crisis.

177
00:06:25,673 --> 00:06:27,742
In March 2023,

178
00:06:27,742 --> 00:06:29,967
Credit Suisse was taken over by its

179
00:06:29,967 --> 00:06:30,835
rival UBS,

180
00:06:30,835 --> 00:06:33,283
in a deal approved by the financial

181
00:06:33,283 --> 00:06:35,953
regulator and backed by the federal

182
00:06:35,953 --> 00:06:38,333
government and the central bank.

183
00:06:43,983 --> 00:06:45,993
But here is the twist.

184
00:06:46,013 --> 00:06:48,655
Look at what Switzerland physically ships

185
00:06:48,655 --> 00:06:49,352
abroad,

186
00:06:49,352 --> 00:06:51,633
and banks barely appear.

187
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:53,540
In 2024,

188
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:56,008
chemical and pharmaceutical products made

189
00:06:56,008 --> 00:06:59,343
up 52.7 percent of Swiss exports.

190
00:06:59,623 --> 00:07:02,117
More than half of everything the country

191
00:07:02,117 --> 00:07:04,758
sold abroad came from one industry.

192
00:07:05,017 --> 00:07:07,303
The centre of gravity is Basel,

193
00:07:07,303 --> 00:07:08,897
a city on the Rhine,

194
00:07:08,897 --> 00:07:10,744
close to the borders with France and

195
00:07:10,744 --> 00:07:12,037
Germany.

196
00:07:12,247 --> 00:07:14,662
The drug makers Roche and Novartis both

197
00:07:14,662 --> 00:07:17,194
have their global headquarters here.

198
00:07:17,450 --> 00:07:20,387
Medicines are almost the perfect product

199
00:07:20,387 --> 00:07:21,342
for a small,

200
00:07:21,342 --> 00:07:22,296
landlocked,

201
00:07:22,296 --> 00:07:24,120
expensive country.

202
00:07:24,150 --> 00:07:25,156
They are light,

203
00:07:25,156 --> 00:07:26,677
they are valuable,

204
00:07:26,677 --> 00:07:29,255
and they are protected by patents and

205
00:07:29,255 --> 00:07:31,660
know-how that take years to build.

206
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:34,488
Switzerland does not compete on what is

207
00:07:34,488 --> 00:07:35,850
cheap to make.

208
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:37,688
It competes on what is hard to

209
00:07:37,688 --> 00:07:38,685
copy.

210
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:40,560
And the watches?

211
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:41,469
They are real,

212
00:07:41,469 --> 00:07:43,700
and they are big business.

213
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,266
In twenty twenty-four,

214
00:07:45,266 --> 00:07:48,470
Swiss watch exports were worth about 29.4

215
00:07:48,470 --> 00:07:49,568
billion dollars,

216
00:07:49,568 --> 00:07:52,167
down 2.8 percent on the year,

217
00:07:52,167 --> 00:07:54,184
according to the watch industry's

218
00:07:54,184 --> 00:07:55,680
federation.

219
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:58,751
It is the same lesson as medicine:

220
00:07:58,751 --> 00:07:59,213
small,

221
00:07:59,213 --> 00:07:59,675
light,

222
00:07:59,675 --> 00:08:02,347
expensive objects that are very hard to

223
00:08:02,347 --> 00:08:03,433
copy.

224
00:08:09,083 --> 00:08:12,662
Hard-to-copy products need people who are

225
00:08:12,662 --> 00:08:14,563
hard to replace.

226
00:08:14,633 --> 00:08:16,763
About two thirds of young people in

227
00:08:16,763 --> 00:08:20,076
Switzerland go into vocational education

228
00:08:20,076 --> 00:08:21,000
and training,

229
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,840
usually apprenticeships that combine the

230
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:27,043
classroom with real work inside a company.

231
00:08:27,293 --> 00:08:28,762
In many countries,

232
00:08:28,762 --> 00:08:31,293
that path is seen as a fallback.

233
00:08:31,293 --> 00:08:31,903
Here,

234
00:08:31,903 --> 00:08:34,108
it is the mainstream.

235
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:36,640
Skills feed ideas.

236
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:38,415
In 2025,

237
00:08:38,415 --> 00:08:40,340
the World Intellectual Property

238
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:42,906
Organization ranked Switzerland first out

239
00:08:42,906 --> 00:08:46,712
of 139 economies in its Global Innovation

240
00:08:46,712 --> 00:08:47,740
Index.

241
00:08:47,910 --> 00:08:48,694
Sweden,

242
00:08:48,694 --> 00:08:50,560
the United States,

243
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,534
South Korea and Singapore completed the

244
00:08:53,534 --> 00:08:54,675
top five.

245
00:08:54,917 --> 00:08:57,887
Skills also keep people in work.

246
00:08:57,987 --> 00:08:59,353
In twenty twenty-four,

247
00:08:59,353 --> 00:09:01,332
the average unemployment rate in

248
00:09:01,332 --> 00:09:03,628
Switzerland was 2.4 percent,

249
00:09:03,628 --> 00:09:06,181
according to the economics ministry,

250
00:09:06,181 --> 00:09:07,177
SECO.

251
00:09:07,527 --> 00:09:08,650
A year earlier,

252
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:10,620
at 2.0 percent,

253
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:12,682
it had been at its lowest level

254
00:09:12,682 --> 00:09:14,657
since two thousand and one.

255
00:09:15,097 --> 00:09:16,885
And the jobs pull people in from

256
00:09:16,885 --> 00:09:18,271
across the borders:

257
00:09:18,271 --> 00:09:22,406
around 403,000 cross-border commuters,

258
00:09:22,406 --> 00:09:24,172
most of them living in France,

259
00:09:24,172 --> 00:09:25,938
Italy and Germany,

260
00:09:25,938 --> 00:09:28,173
were working in Switzerland at the end

261
00:09:28,173 --> 00:09:30,785
of September twenty twenty-four.

262
00:09:36,450 --> 00:09:38,957
Now for the part that rarely makes

263
00:09:38,957 --> 00:09:40,530
the brochures.

264
00:09:40,530 --> 00:09:42,296
Success is expensive,

265
00:09:42,296 --> 00:09:43,920
literally.

266
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:46,153
In 2024,

267
00:09:46,153 --> 00:09:48,675
Switzerland had the highest price level of

268
00:09:48,675 --> 00:09:51,404
all the countries compared by Eurostat,

269
00:09:51,404 --> 00:09:55,260
almost 60 percent above the EU average.

270
00:09:55,517 --> 00:09:58,182
High prices and a strong franc squeeze

271
00:09:58,182 --> 00:10:00,848
anyone who has to compete from

272
00:10:00,848 --> 00:10:02,447
Switzerland.

273
00:10:02,687 --> 00:10:04,933
That pressure is exactly what the central

274
00:10:04,933 --> 00:10:09,577
bank was fighting between 2011 and 2015.

275
00:10:09,827 --> 00:10:12,342
Being a safe haven is a privilege,

276
00:10:12,342 --> 00:10:14,649
but the bill arrives in the export

277
00:10:14,649 --> 00:10:15,710
sector.

278
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:16,401
Then,

279
00:10:16,401 --> 00:10:18,038
in twenty twenty-five,

280
00:10:18,038 --> 00:10:20,174
the risk of depending on a few

281
00:10:20,174 --> 00:10:22,910
big customers became very real.

282
00:10:23,190 --> 00:10:24,509
From August seventh,

283
00:10:24,509 --> 00:10:27,193
Swiss exports to the United States faced

284
00:10:27,193 --> 00:10:29,850
a tariff of 39 percent.

285
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:32,659
The Swiss government called it unjustified

286
00:10:32,659 --> 00:10:35,350
and a heavy burden for exporters.

287
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:36,841
In November,

288
00:10:36,841 --> 00:10:38,986
the two countries signed a non-binding

289
00:10:38,986 --> 00:10:40,101
memorandum:

290
00:10:40,101 --> 00:10:42,504
the extra tariff would fall to 15

291
00:10:42,504 --> 00:10:43,126
percent,

292
00:10:43,126 --> 00:10:45,249
in line with the European Union,

293
00:10:45,249 --> 00:10:48,209
and Swiss companies planned 200 billion

294
00:10:48,209 --> 00:10:50,998
dollars of investment in the United States

295
00:10:50,998 --> 00:10:53,314
by the end of twenty twenty-eight.

296
00:10:53,550 --> 00:10:56,980
And concentration cuts both ways.

297
00:10:57,110 --> 00:10:58,938
When more than half of your exports

298
00:10:58,938 --> 00:11:00,514
come from one industry,

299
00:11:00,514 --> 00:11:02,571
a shock to that industry is a

300
00:11:02,571 --> 00:11:04,770
shock to the whole country.

301
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:07,878
The Credit Suisse rescue was a reminder

302
00:11:07,878 --> 00:11:10,396
that a great financial centre can also

303
00:11:10,396 --> 00:11:12,759
become a national risk.

304
00:11:18,417 --> 00:11:18,908
So,

305
00:11:18,908 --> 00:11:21,317
why is Switzerland so rich?

306
00:11:21,397 --> 00:11:23,246
Not because of what lies under its

307
00:11:23,246 --> 00:11:23,637
ground,

308
00:11:23,637 --> 00:11:25,281
but because of what it built on

309
00:11:25,281 --> 00:11:26,467
top of it.

310
00:11:26,467 --> 00:11:28,670
Stability that investors trust,

311
00:11:28,670 --> 00:11:31,540
earned over two centuries and defended,

312
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:33,697
sometimes at great cost.

313
00:11:33,777 --> 00:11:36,041
A financial system that had to reinvent

314
00:11:36,041 --> 00:11:37,894
itself when secrecy stopped being

315
00:11:37,894 --> 00:11:39,317
acceptable.

316
00:11:39,397 --> 00:11:41,463
Specialisation in products that are hard

317
00:11:41,463 --> 00:11:42,627
to copy.

318
00:11:42,627 --> 00:11:44,917
And skills that keep them that way.

319
00:11:45,017 --> 00:11:47,397
None of these forces is guaranteed.

320
00:11:47,397 --> 00:11:48,719
A strong currency,

321
00:11:48,719 --> 00:11:50,960
high prices and a dependence on a

322
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:52,529
few giants are the price of the

323
00:11:52,529 --> 00:11:53,577
model.

324
00:11:53,687 --> 00:11:55,609
The question for the next decade is

325
00:11:55,609 --> 00:11:57,532
whether a small country can keep it

326
00:11:57,532 --> 00:11:59,433
running in a world of bigger,

327
00:11:59,433 --> 00:12:00,887
louder powers.

328
00:12:00,917 --> 00:12:02,067
Thanks for watching.

329
00:12:02,067 --> 00:12:04,118
This is The Hidden Economics.
